{%- set faqs = [
  ["What is post-quantum cryptography?", "Post-quantum cryptography (PQC) is a family of encryption and digital signature algorithms designed to stay secure even against attacks from large quantum computers. Unlike quantum key distribution, it runs on ordinary hardware and software, so organizations can adopt it through updates to their existing systems."],
  ["Why does quantum risk matter if large quantum computers do not exist yet?", "Attackers can record encrypted traffic and stolen data today and decrypt it later, once a capable quantum computer exists. This is often called harvest now, decrypt later. Data that must stay confidential for many years, such as health, financial, legal and government records, is already exposed to that risk."],
  ["When will quantum computers break today's encryption?", "Nobody can give a reliable date. What is clear is that migrating large organizations takes years, and standards bodies have already published algorithms and timelines. Planning now means you finish on your own schedule instead of reacting to someone else's."],
  ["Which cryptographic algorithms are vulnerable to quantum attacks?", "Public-key algorithms based on integer factoring and discrete logarithms are the main concern. That includes RSA, Diffie-Hellman, elliptic-curve Diffie-Hellman and ECDSA. They are used for key exchange, certificates and digital signatures across TLS, VPNs, PKI, code signing and many other systems."],
  ["Is AES encryption safe against quantum computers?", "Symmetric encryption such as AES is affected much less. Current guidance is to use 256-bit keys for long-term protection, and to use strong hash functions such as SHA-384 or SHA-512 where appropriate. The larger migration effort is in the public-key algorithms."],
  ["What are the NIST post-quantum standards?", "In August 2024 NIST published the first three post-quantum standards: FIPS 203 (ML-KEM) for key establishment, FIPS 204 (ML-DSA) for digital signatures and FIPS 205 (SLH-DSA) for hash-based signatures. NIST has also proposed a timeline for deprecating and then disallowing quantum-vulnerable algorithms, with 2030 and 2035 as the key dates in its draft."],
  ["What is crypto-agility?", "Crypto-agility is the ability to change cryptographic algorithms, keys and libraries without redesigning your systems. Organizations that can swap algorithms through configuration and updates will migrate faster, and will also be better prepared for the next algorithm change, whatever causes it."],
  ["What is a cryptographic inventory?", "A cryptographic inventory is a list of where cryptography is used in your environment: which algorithms, key sizes and certificates protect which systems, who owns them and what depends on them. It is the foundation of any migration, because you cannot replace what you cannot find."],
  ["What is a hybrid approach to post-quantum migration?", "A hybrid approach combines a classical algorithm with a post-quantum one, so a connection stays protected if either is later found to be weak. Many organizations use hybrid key exchange as a first step because it adds quantum protection without dropping the classical safeguards they rely on today."],
  ["How long does a post-quantum migration take?", "It depends on the size of your estate, the number of third-party dependencies and how much of your cryptography is built into hardware or old software. Discovery and planning can take weeks to a few months. Full migration of a large organization is a multi-year program, which is why it should be phased."],
  ["Do small and mid-sized businesses need to prepare?", "Yes, though the effort is smaller. Most of your cryptography comes from vendors, cloud platforms and browsers, so the priority is knowing what you depend on, asking vendors about their quantum-safe roadmaps and protecting any data that must stay confidential for a long time."],
  ["Do you implement the changes or only advise?", "Our service focuses on assessment, planning and review: finding where you are exposed, prioritizing it and defining the migration path, then reviewing pilots and implementations. Your engineering team or vendors carry out the changes, and we support them with specific guidance."],
  ["How much does a post-quantum cryptography assessment cost?", "Cost depends on the size of your environment, the number of systems and vendors in scope and the depth of the inventory. Share a short description of your environment with CipherTrivia and we will recommend a scope and quote."],
  ["What does the assessment report include?", "The report includes an executive summary, a cryptographic inventory, a quantum risk register that ranks systems by exposure and data lifetime, crypto-agility findings and a phased migration roadmap with recommended owners and priorities."]
] %}

