<section class="section section--white" aria-labelledby="s1-title">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="split__aside">
        <h2 class="split__title rv" id="s1-title">Quantum-Safe Security Starts With Knowing Where Your Cryptography Is</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="split__body prose">
        <p class="rv">Almost every secure connection, signed update, certificate and encrypted record relies on public-key cryptography such as RSA and elliptic curves. A sufficiently powerful quantum computer could break those algorithms. No one can say when that will happen, but attackers can already collect encrypted data now and decrypt it later, so information that must stay private for years is exposed today.</p>
        <p class="rv">Standards bodies have responded. NIST has published its first post-quantum standards and proposed a timeline for retiring vulnerable algorithms. For most organizations the hard part is not choosing an algorithm. It is finding every place cryptography is used, understanding what depends on it and deciding what to change first.</p>
        <p class="rv">CipherTrivia's post-quantum cryptography services give you that picture. We inventory your cryptography, assess quantum risk against how long your data must stay confidential, review your crypto-agility and build a phased roadmap your teams and vendors can follow.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

