<!-- ================================================================== -->
<section class="section section--stone" aria-labelledby="s4-title">
  <div class="container">
    <header class="shead">
      <div class="shead__meta"></div>
      <div class="shead__body">
        <h2 class="shead__title rv" id="s4-title">Algorithms and Standards We Work With</h2>
        <p class="shead__lede rv" style="--d:1">Our recommendations follow published standards, not vendor claims. Here is how the landscape looks, and how it shapes the plans we write.</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <ul class="includes">
      <li class="include rv">{{ icon("unlock", "sm") }}<p><strong>Algorithms at Risk:</strong> RSA, Diffie-Hellman, elliptic-curve Diffie-Hellman and ECDSA, which protect key exchange, certificates and signatures and are the focus of migration.</p></li>
      <li class="include rv" style="--d:1">{{ icon("key", "sm") }}<p><strong>ML-KEM (FIPS 203):</strong> NIST's standard for establishing shared keys, the main replacement for today's key exchange.</p></li>
      <li class="include rv" style="--d:2">{{ icon("badge", "sm") }}<p><strong>ML-DSA (FIPS 204):</strong> NIST's standard for digital signatures, intended for certificates, software signing and authentication.</p></li>
      <li class="include rv">{{ icon("doc-check", "sm") }}<p><strong>SLH-DSA (FIPS 205):</strong> A hash-based signature standard that offers a different security basis as a conservative alternative.</p></li>
      <li class="include rv" style="--d:1">{{ icon("shield-check", "sm") }}<p><strong>Symmetric Encryption and Hashing:</strong> AES with 256-bit keys and strong hash functions, which need far less change than public-key algorithms.</p></li>
      <li class="include rv" style="--d:2">{{ icon("layers", "sm") }}<p><strong>Hybrid Approaches:</strong> Combining classical and post-quantum algorithms, often the practical first step while standards and products mature.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

